# Intro
This example simulates the gravitational collapse of a turbulent molecular
cloud: a uniform-density sphere seeded with a turbulent velocity field drawn
from a power-law power spectrum. It is a standard test for sink particle
formation in a turbulence-dominated (rather than rotation-dominated, see
../BossBodenheimer) collapse.

`--solenoidal_fraction` sets, exactly (not just on average), the fraction of
the turbulent kinetic energy that is solenoidal (divergence-free) rather than
compressive (curl-free): 1.0 is purely solenoidal, 0.0 purely compressive,
and 2/3 reproduces the natural, unprojected isotropic mixture of the two (a
raw random 3-vector field has 2 of its 3 degrees of freedom transverse).
Note: an older, pNbody-based version of this generator had a
`--velocity_field_type` option with labels `solenoidal`/`compressive`/`mixed`,
but its outputs were empirically indistinguishable (all had the isotropic
~1/3 longitudinal energy fraction expected of an *unprojected* field) -- the
projection was not actually taking effect. This generator's projection was
verified directly against the expected 0 / 1 / 1/3 longitudinal energy
fractions before being used here; see makeIC.py's module docstring.

The default resolution (N=100000) is chosen to run in a reasonable time on a
laptop; the run this example was originally validated against used N=4e6,
which takes considerably longer both to generate and to run. Raise `-N` (and
`--N_mesh` alongside it, so the turbulent field stays at least as resolved as
the particle sampling) for production/paper-quality runs.

# Configure
To run this example with the GEAR model,

    ./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-feedback=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle

and then

    make -j

# ICs
The run.sh script calls `makeIC.py` with default values. Run
`python3 makeIC.py --help` to see all the available options.

# Run
Type `./run.sh` (`N=<value>` and `solenoidal_fraction=<0..1>` override the
defaults), and let's go!

Sink particles form and accrete gas in this test, but do not spawn stars
(`GEARSink:disable_star_formation: 1` in params.yml) -- this test targets
sink formation/accretion physics, not the stellar feedback loop.
